==Electoral prospects: Senate==
The coalition go in to the election with 39 of 76 seats, a one seat majority. Labor have 28 seats, Greens four, Democrats four, and Family First one. A total of 36 senators are not up for re-election: 19 from the Coalition, 14 from Labor, two Greens and one Family First. The Coalition needs to win 20 of the 40 contested Senate seats to maintain its Senate majority, while Labor would need to win 25 seats to have a Senate majority in its own right. A Labor majority would require a preferred vote of over 57 percent in five of the six states; this would be a record for Labor, surpassing the 55.09 percent it recorded in the [[Australian federal election, 1943|1943 election]]. On recent polling, it appears likely that the balance of power will revert to the minor parties.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.smh.com.au/articles/2007/06/26/1182623896172.html|title=Govt warns against Greens after poll|date=[[26 June]] [[2007]]|work=Sydney Morning Herald|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

The minor parties and independents considered to have the best chance of winning Senate seats are the [[Australian Greens]], [[Family First]], the [[Pauline's United Australia Party|Pauline Hanson group]] and independent [[Nick Xenophon]] in South Australia<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theage.com.au/news/national/last-chance-for-democrats-to-assert-their-relevance/2007/10/17/1192300859173.html|title=Last chance for Democrats to assert their relevance|last=Dubecki|first=Larissa|work=[[The Age]]|date=[[18 October]] [[2007]]}}</ref>. There is also an outside chance of the [[New South Wales|NSW]] [[Christian Democratic Party (Australia)|CDP]] or [[Liberty & Democracy Party|LDP]] candidates winning a seat through preference harvesting <ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.upperhouse.info/ArchiveView.aspx?EntryHeadId=159|title=ABC Senate Calculators|date=[[5 November]] [[2007]]}}</ref>. The [[Australian Democrats]], who had the greatest number of senators among the minor parties, from 1978 to 2005, have been widely predicted to lose all their seats. All four Democrat seats are up for re-election, with two incumbents, [[Andrew Murray (politician)|Andrew Murray]] and [[Natasha Stott Despoja]], deciding to retire upon the expiry of their term.<ref>{{cite [http://www.roymorgan.com/news/polls/2007/4227/ Finding No. 4227 - Latest Morgan Senate Poll: October 18, 2007]</ref><ref name=nopokies>{{cite news|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2007/10/12/2057774.htm|title=No Pokies MP odds-on for Senate seat|date=[[12 October]] [[2007]]|work=ABC News Online|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theage.com.au/articles/2007/10/17/1192300859185.html|title=Recognise these men? They may hold balance of power|work=The Age|date=[[18 October]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref> Each group's success will largely depend on securing favourable preference flows from the major parties and assorted micro-parties. While the Coalition concluded a preference deal with Family First, Labor chose instead to make a preference arrangement with the Greens.<ref>[http://www.news.com.au/story/0,23599,22656009-5012863,00.html]</ref> [[One Nation (Australia)|One Nation]] preferences will favour the Liberals over Labor in all states except Victoria, whilst Pauline Hanson's party has preferenced the crucial 3rd Labor seat before the 3rd Liberal/National seat in both Queensland and New South Wales.<ref>[http://www.news.com.au/story/0,23599,22701712-5012863,00.html]</ref><ref>{{cite 
news|url=http://www.theage.com.au/articles/2007/09/22/1189881833182.html|title=So, is the party over?|last=Koutsoukis|first=Jason|work=The Age|date=[[23 September]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>  

Coalition senators [[Paul Calvert]], [[Rod Kemp]], [[Sandy Macdonald]] and [[Kay Patterson]]; and Labor senator [[Robert Ray (Australian politician)|Robert Ray]] will not contest the 2007 election.

===High-profile candidates===
(<!-- Please note that, to avoid the appearance of promoting candidates here, this list is sorted -->Alphabetically by surname.<!-- Please keep it that way -->)

* Former Queensland politician [[Pauline Hanson]] announced recently that she will be standing for the Senate in Queensland. <ref> [http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2007/08/15/2005271.htm "Hanson Launches new political party."], ''[[Australian Broadcasting Corporation|ABC]]'', [[August 15]], [[2007]]/ Accessed [[August}}, [[2007]].</ref> Recent polling indicates her primary vote to be around 7.5 percent, but it is expected that she will stuggle to get the preferences required to gain a seat.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.smh.com.au/articles/2007/10/19/1192301026185.html|title=Hanson attracting 7.5% support in Qld|work=Sydney Morning Herald|date=[[19 October]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

* Author and broadcaster Dr [[Karl Kruszelnicki]] is running for the Senate in New South Wales for the [[Climate Change Coalition]], occupying second place on the party's ticket in that state behind [[Patrice Newell]].

* [[Andrew Wilkie]] has been pre-selected as second on the ticket for the [[Tasmanian Greens]] behind Senator [[Bob Brown]]. He is a former [[intelligence officer]] who resigned from the [[Office of National Assessments (Australia)|Office of National Assessments]] (ONA) over his concerns about government use of intelligence information in building a case for the [[2003 invasion of Iraq]]. In the [[Australian federal election, 2004|2004 election]] he polled 16% for the [[Australian Greens|Greens]] in Prime Minister [[John Howard]]'s electorate of [[Division of Bennelong|Bennelong]].

* [[Nick Xenophon]], a [[No Pokies]] MP in the [[Parliament of South Australia]], resigned to run for a South Australian Senate seat. He attracted a fifth of the statewide upper house vote (20.5 percent) at the [[South Australian general election, 2006|2006 state election]], and it is widely predicted that he will win a Senate seat<ref name=nopokies />, and possibly hold the [[Balance of power (politics)|balance of power]].<ref>http://www.news.com.au/adelaidenow/story/0,22606,22571626-5006301,00.html</ref> His platform will consist of anti-gambling, pro-[[consumer protection]], attention to the water crisis, signing the [[Kyoto protocol]], support opposition against what he calls a decrease in state rights, and opposition to [[WorkChoices]].<ref>http://www.theaustralian.news.com.au/story/0,25197,22567900-5006787,00.html</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theage.com.au/articles/2007/10/11/1191696082652.html|title=Fearful of Xenophon in Senate|last=Debelle|first=Penelope|work=The Age|date=[[12 October]] [[2007]]|accessdate=2007-10-22}}</ref>

==Television coverage==
The 2007 Federal Election will be covered extensively, commercial-free, by three major Australian free-to-air networks: [[ABC Television]], the [[Nine Network]] and the [[Seven Network]].

*Nine's coverage begins at 6.30 p.m. and follows through until 8.30 p.m. It will be hosted by journalist [[Ray Martin]] and Nine's political editor [[Laurie Oakes]]. Special guests include Labor politicians Robert Ray and Treasurer Wayne Swan, Liberals Michael Kroger and Communications minister Helen Coonan and John Howard's biographer [[Peter Van Onselen]] with light hearted views from the tally room coming from Chaser stuntman [[Charles Firth]]. Special reporters include [[Peter Overton]], [[Tracy Grimshaw]] and [[Kellie Connolly]]. 

*Seven will be going with a new election coverage team for 2007, led by breakfast television hosts [[David Koch]] and [[Melissa Doyle]]. There coverage also runs from 6.30 p.m. but has no planned finish time. Special guests include current and former Liberal politicians [[Joe Hockey]] (Work Choices minister), and [[Jackie Kelly]]. Joining them will be Labor's [[Tanya Plibersek]] and the National Party's [[Barnaby Joyce]]. Special Reporters include [[Anna Coren]], [[Mark Riley]] and [[Chris Bath]].    

*The ABC's coverage will begin at 6 p.m., led by Kerry O'Brien, Tony Jones and Antony Green. Special guests include Senator [[Nick Minchin]] (finance minister), representing the Coalition, and [[Julia Gillard]] (deputy ALP leader) from the ALP. Special Reporters include editors [[Jim Middleton]] and [[Michael Brissenden]], and other journalists with the leaders in Sydney and Brisbane. The ABC will provide live crosses to all the key electorates around Australia.

- [[Network Ten]] and [[SBS]] will include brief updates and news bulletins through the night, but not to the other networks extent. <ref>{{cite news |first= David|last= Knox|title= Election Night: Who's Got What|url= http://www.tvtonight.com.au/2007/11/election-night-whos-got-what.html|work= blog|publisher= TV Tonight |date= 9 November 2007 |accessdate=2007-11-09 }}</ref>

